Open-Rank Teaching Faculty Position in Pharmacology
Department of Pharmacology
UT Health San Antonio
The Department of Pharmacology in the Joe R. Teresa Lozano Long School of Medicine at UT Health San Antonio invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor. Academic rank will be commensurate with the candidate’s qualifications and experience.
The successful candidate will serve as a primary teaching faculty member within the Department of Pharmacology. This is a teaching-intensive appointment involving direct instruction, course leadership, curriculum development, assessment, and educational service across multiple professional and graduate programs.
Position Responsibilities
The successful candidate will be expected to:
• Provide instruction in foundational, systems-based, and clinically applied pharmacology;
• Serve as course director, co-director, or principal faculty member in courses serving students in Medicine, Dentistry, Nursing, and Health Professions, and graduate biomedical science programs;
• Teach topics that may include pharmacodynamics, pharmacokinetics, pharmacotherapeutics, toxicology, drug discovery, and organ-system pharmacology;
• Contribute to curriculum development, program evaluation, accreditation activities, and educational quality improvements;
• Participate in department and institutional educational service;
Based on experience and rank, the faculty member may assume leadership roles in educational initiatives and curriculum governance and participate in educational scholarship or collaborative research.
Required Qualifications
Applicants must have:
• A doctoral degree in pharmacology, pharmaceutical sciences, clinical pharmacology, pharmacy, biomedical sciences, or a closely related discipline. Appropriate terminal degrees may include a PhD, PharmD, MD, or equivalent degree;
• Experience teaching pharmacology or a closely related biomedical discipline at the university level;
• The ability to communicate complex pharmacological concepts effectively to learners with varied educational and professional backgrounds;
Experience directing courses, teaching across professional or graduate programs, developing curricula or assessments, or providing educational leadership is preferred.
Faculty Advancement & Environment
Faculty members are eligible for promotion in academic rank based on sustained accomplishment in teaching and educational leadership in accordance with departmental and university guidelines. Junior faculty will receive mentorship in developing an academic portfolio appropriate for advancement. The Department of Pharmacology supports education across biomedical graduate and health-professions programs and maintains a collegial environment committed to excellence in teaching, research, mentorship, and service. Additional information is available at https://lsom.uthscsa.edu/pharmacology/ .
Application Instructions
Applicants should submit:
• A cover letter describing their qualifications and interest in the position;
• A current curriculum vitae;
• A statement of teaching philosophy and experience;
• A description of prior course leadership, curriculum development, or educational innovation, as applicable; and
• Contact information for three professional references.
Apply at https://wp.uthscsa.edu/careers/ using REQ# 26000193.
Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled.
UT Health San Antonio is an Equal Employment Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer including protected veterans and persons with disabilities. All faculty appointments are designated as security sensitive positions.
Additional Information

Health Sciences Research Faculty Position
The University of Texas Health Science Center San Antonio , in partnership with the search firm Scott Healy & Associates , is pleased to announce a national search for an accomplished Researcher in the Health Sciences . Reporting to the Associate Dean for Research, the Open Rank Research Faculty Member will have a full-time tenure or tenure-track faculty appointment, depending on education, experience and related qualifications. The School of Health Professions seeks an individual with a history of successful external funding capable of engaging in collaborative research in an area aligned with the School of Health Professions and University.
Essential Duties
• Secure research grant funding in a health-related discipline (e.g. health/healthcare outcomes research; rehabilitation sciences; acute care; chronic disease management; community health; population health; data analytics; applied physiology, pathology or medical laboratory sciences; or other discipline specific research focus).
• Conduct healthcare research that supports departmental, school, and university missions;
• Publish and disseminate research to national and international audiences;
• Collaborate and create supportive partnerships both within and outside the program, school, and University.
• Mentor/supervise doctoral student research in research area focus, as assigned.
Qualifications
• Possess an earned doctorate relevant to a School of Health Professions department with experience as an independent researcher;
• Successful track record of obtaining grant funding, evidence of active grant funding that is portable preferred;
• Ability to work independently and as an effective team member;
• Evidence of publication in peer refereed journals;
• Excellent communication skills.
University of Texas Health Science Center San Antonio: UT Health
The University of Texas Health Science Center at San Antonio is one of the country’s leading academic health centers. UT Health ranks among the top health science universities worldwide in receiving National Institutes of Health (NIH) funding and has provided outstanding health science education and training since its founding in 1968.
The mission of UT Health San Antonio is to make lives better through excellence in education, research, health care and community engagement. Strategies for achieving this mission include:
• Educating a diverse student body to become excellent health care providers and scientists;
• Engaging in research to understand health and disease;
• Providing compassionate and culturally proficient health care;
• Engaging our community to improve health;
• Influencing thoughtful advances in health policy.
In fulfillment of this mission, UT Health San Antonio seeks to provide the best in health career education, conduct groundbreaking biomedical research, while also providing the best patient care and community service to San Antonio and the South Texas/Border Region. With undergraduate, graduate and postgraduate programs, UT Health San Antonio is a major academic health science university composed of five schools: School of Medicine, School of Health Professions, School of Nursing, Dental School, and Graduate School of Biomedical Sciences. For more information on UT Health visit our website at uthscsa.edu .
The School of Health Professions
As an integral part of UT Health San Antonio, the School of Health Professions (SHP) seeks to make lives better through excellence in teaching, research and scholarship, service and patient care. The SHP strives to educate a diverse student body in becoming outstanding health care practitioners, scientists and leaders; advance health research and scholarship; and provide health services and education to the community.
The SHP offers graduate degrees in Medical Laboratory Sciences (MS), Physician Assistant Studies (MPAS), Respiratory Care (MS) and Speech Language Pathology (MS) and advanced training through doctoral degree program
